Subject: Evenly distributed data
Message-ID: <Dvsj0JoyLr4.M6q5HVMsDgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/Dvsj0JoyLr4/m/M6q5HVMsDgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

Thank you for providing such fantastic online resources and for all the help you have already provided on this list. I am a PhD student at Manchester Metropolitan University researching the population density and abundance of Northern lesser galagos (Galago senegalensis) in Senegal (37 transects; range: 554–4519 m) and Kenya (1059 transects; range: 1059–11765m) alongside my colleagues at MMU. We are analysing the data from the two populations separately.

We segmented the data from both populations using the ‘rule of thumb’ (2 x truncation distance). This created 512 segments of roughly 160 m in length for Sengal and 506 segments of roughly 200 m in length for Kenya. We then ran simple dsms (no covariates), which seemed to work well for the Kenya data (~49 % deviance explained) but only ~4 % deviance was explained for the dsm on the Senegal data. The Senegal data is much more evenly distributed than the Kenya data – could that be the reason for the poor model in Senegal? We tried other distributions (eg. tweedie) and there was no improvement. We’ve increased the segment size from 160m (2 x truncation distance of 80m) to 300m for the Senegal data and there is a slight improvement but not much difference.

Any advice you have that could improve the performance of the model(s) would be much appreciated.

All the best,

Grace

From uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com  Fri Jul 12 11:15:05 2019
From: uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com (Eric Rexstad)
Date: Fri, 12 Jul 2019 11:15:05 +0000
Subject: Re: [distance-sampling] Number of Detections Per Stratum - Line Transect Survey Design
Message-ID: <UekF-aq5knA.xJe8WgQ1BwAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <UekF-aq5knA.sWDijbUUDgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <UekF-aq5knA.sWDijbUUDgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/UekF-aq5knA/m/xJe8WgQ1BwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Elaine

All valuable things to consider when designing a distance sampling survey.  If you have a copy of Buckland et al. (2015) to hand, have a look at Section 2.3 for a discussion of the subject.

In short, you can use stratification to accomplish one of two purposes: a) estimate density in sub-areas (strata) of your study area or b) increase precision of your estimate across the entire study area as you note.  The suggested sample sizes for number of detections and number of transects applies to the area at which you are performing the estimation, in your case the entire study area.

Regarding effort allocation based upon presumed densities:  it is common to allocate greater effort in areas of higher density.  There are drawbacks to this allocation scheme.  With low effort in areas of low density, estimates of abundance in these areas will be imprecise.  That might not be a problem if the low-density areas are small in size.  But if low density areas are large (high quality habitat is scarce) then these low density areas might constitute a large proportion of the population in the study area (and it will be poorly estimated) (Section 2.3.1 Buckland et al. (2015)).  The result of the effort allocation scheme is unlikely to produce bias in the point estimates, but there the situation as I have described could cause precision to suffer.

If the answer to this question is critical to your investigation, you might consider conducting some contrasting simulations in Distance for Windows or the DSsim R package.
Buckland, S. T., E. A. Rexstad, T. A. Marques, and C. S. Oedekoven. 2015. Distance Sampling: Methods and Applications. Springer.

